# Presentation of the Supplementary Budget for 1968-69
Tamil title: 1968-69ஆம் ஆண்டிற்கான துணை நிதிநிலை அறிக்கை அளித்தல்

**The Hon. Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** Mr. Chairman, I have come before you to place the first supplementary estimates for 1968-69 before this House. A detailed statement explaining the demands for grants has been laid before the House. These supplementary demands total Rs. 6.40 crore, of which Rs. 3.32 crore falls under the revenue account. They include the “new service” schemes sanctioned after the current year's Budget was placed before the Legislature last February. We have also used this opportunity to seek additional provision under certain grants where expenditure is now expected to exceed the amount initially provided in the Budget.

The largest additional liability reflected in these estimates arises from the increase in dearness allowance rates sanctioned with effect from 1 June of the current year. As a result, the dearness allowance of our employees now corresponds to that of Central Government employees in the same grades. As usual, this increase also applies to local-body employees and teachers in aided schools. The resulting additional net expenditure during the current year is estimated at Rs. 2.70 crore. The necessary additional provision has been sought under the different grants. We expect the Fifth Finance Commission, while determining our State's financial needs, to take this essential liability into account and make adequate financial provision for our State in its interim report expected in September.

I wish to remind hon. members that, in my Budget speech, I referred to the Government's major policy decision to obtain institutional credit on a large scale for implementing the extensive food-production schemes now under way. We have appointed special staff at district level to implement a new scheme through which the credit needs of cultivators can be met easily and efficiently by land mortgage banks. Provision has been made for this under Demands 16 and 19.

Since special development schemes have also been undertaken for South Arcot and Chengalpattu districts, additional provision is sought under Demand 16. The total investment in these schemes is estimated at more than Rs. 2 crore. Under them, 2,000 private tube wells and point tube wells will be sunk in South Arcot district, and 2,000 private tube wells and three hundred point tube wells in Chengalpattu district. We must undertake such schemes to develop new and substantial sources and make full use of the groundwater available in these areas. We must also implement swiftly the connected programmes for extending electric power to irrigation.

There had been persistent complaints that private electricity licensees in Thanjavur district had, for various reasons, shown insufficient interest in rural electrification schemes. Consequently, this region, which plays an important part in our State's agricultural economy, lagged behind the rest of the State in using electricity for lift irrigation. Members of this House know that we issued notices to take over the undertakings of the private licensees operating in this district.

Of these, the Mayuram-Mannargudi-Tiruvarur licensed undertaking has already come under State Government control. Seventy-five per cent of the compensation legally due to it must be paid shortly. A provision of Rs. 74 lakh is therefore sought in these supplementary estimates toward the estimated cost of the takeover.

Although it is proper to give special importance to the full use of minor-irrigation sources, we must not forget the importance of maintaining major-irrigation schemes properly at the same time. In light of the experience gained from introducing the new cropping pattern in the Thanjavur delta, the Government has sanctioned improvements to the Arasalar anicut and the Cauvery irrigation arrangements so that the new pattern may assuredly succeed. A sum of Rs. 26 lakh has been provided under Grant 25 for the expenditure expected on this scheme during the current year.

I am confident that the House will welcome the additional provision of Rs. 50 lakh for loans to cultivators to purchase fertilisers. With this provision, the total amount provided for the current year is Rs. 2 crore…

[The source loses the remainder of this sentence and the transition to the next subject.]

I trust the House will be pleased to know that vigorous measures are being taken to provide the basic facilities needed for the rapid growth of various industries at Manali. The water-supply scheme for the Madras Oil Refinery, being carried out at an approximate cost of Rs. 58 lakh, is progressing rapidly. We have now sanctioned a scheme estimated at Rs. 1.82 crore to supply sixty lakh gallons of water daily to the Madras Fertilizer Factory being established at Manali. A substantial provision of Rs. 1.05 crore is sought under Grant 42 for expenditure likely to be incurred during the current year. The entire cost of this scheme will be met through special loan assistance from the Government of India.

Large numbers of refugees will soon be repatriated from Ceylon. We have drawn up a scheme to grant them loans so that they may pursue occupations suited to their abilities. A provision of Rs. 10 lakh is sought for this scheme under Grant 47. This provision may have to be increased over time according to actual needs. Steps have been taken to increase the number of administrative staff entrusted with this important work and to streamline the procedures. Provision has been made for this under Grant 44.

I am confident that hon. members will welcome the amendment of the relevant rules so that grants toward expenditure on high schools administered by the Madras Corporation and other municipalities may be given more liberally. Instead of reimbursing only fifty per cent of their net deficit as hitherto, the State Government now proposes to reimburse 66⅔ per cent. Provision has been sought under Grant 18. Since the Government must also meet a substantial share of the net deficits of private engineering colleges and polytechnics, the required provision has been included under the same grant. From 1967-68, the State Government's share in this regard has been increased from 66⅔ per cent to seventy-five per cent.

I trust the House will be pleased to learn that the Government proposes to institute annual awards for full-length Tamil films, with a view to encouraging the production of Tamil films of high artistic merit and technical quality. Films produced in 1967 will receive awards during the current year in the form of small-savings certificates totalling Rs. 1.10 lakh. Provision has accordingly been made under Demand 12.

The Government has also begun a scheme to produce a monthly newsreel depicting important events in our State. Provision for this has been made under Grant 9.

I submit that the other schemes included in these supplementary estimates require no special explanation, and request the House to accept these first supplementary estimates for 1968-69.

---NOTES---
The last line of Image 3 ends after “the total amount provided for the current year is Rs. 2 crore,” while Image 4 begins with industrial infrastructure at Manali; the missing transition is explicitly marked and not reconstructed. Several grant numbers are degraded in OCR: the refugee-loan and administrative-staff references are read conservatively as Grants 47 and 44 from the visible numerals. The Mayuram-Mannargudi-Tiruvarur undertaking and Arasalar anicut are rendered from context while the verbatim Tamil OCR remains unchanged.
